    "ls|LS16_S10_U_11": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LAW AND JUSTICE \nDEPARTMENT OF JUSTICE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.11 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY THE 16TH NOVEMBER, 2016. \n \nExtension of Retirement Age for Judges \n \n \n11.   SHRI RAMSINH RATHWA : \n \nWill the Minister of LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether any proposal for extension of retirement age of High Court Judges is under  \nconsideration of Government; \n   (b)   if so, the details thereof;  \n   (c)   whether Standing Committee on PPG and Law and Justice has recommended the  \n          extension of the retirement age of High Court Judges from  62 to 65 years; and  \n   (d)   if so, the reaction of the Government in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LAW AND JUSTICE AND ELECTRONICS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY  \n (SHRI P.P. CHAUDHARY)  \n \n(a) to (d) :  The Constitution (One Hundred and Fourteenth Amendment) Bill, 2010 which \nprovided for increasing the retirement age of High Court Judges from 62 years to 65 years was \nintroduced in the Lok Sabha on 25.08.2010. Ther eafter, the Bill was referred to the \nParliamentary Standing Committee for examination  and  report, which recommended that the \nproposed Bill in its present form should be passed without delay. The Bill could not be taken \nup for consideration and passing in  the Parliament and lapsed with the dissolution of the 15 th \nLok Sabha.  ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_110": "�\n������������������� �\n�������������������������������������� �\n�\n��������� �\n��������������������� ���� �\n������������������� ������������\n�\n������������������� �\n���� �������� �������������������� ��\n������������������� �\n�\n���������������������������������������������������������������������� �\n(a) whether 8 per cent population and 19 per cent area of the country have not been \ncovered under Doordarshan network in the country, so far;  \n \n(b) if so, the reasons therefor along with the names of such areas;  \n \n(c) the time by which coverage of Doordarshan network is likely to be extended \nacross the country; and  \n \n(d) the efforts made to utilise modern techniques and equipments in Doordarshan \ntelecast? \n�\n������ �\n�\n��������������������������������������������� �\n����������������������� ��\n  \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\n�\n������������� ��������������������������������� ������������������\n���� ���������������� ����� ��������������� ������������\n \nPrasar Bharati has informed that in terrestrial mode, the TV coverage of \nDoordarshan is estimated to be available to about 92% population spread over 81% \narea of the country. However, in Satellite mode, multichannel TV coverage of \n'RRUGDUVKDQ\u0003UHDFKHV\u0003DOO\u0003WKH\u0003DUHDV\u0003RI\u0003WKH\u0003FRXQWU\\\u0003WKURXJK\u0003'RRUGDUVKDQ¶V\u0003IUHH-to-air \nDTH service. It is possible to receive DTH signals anywhere in the country with the \nhelp of a small sized dish receive unit.  \n Modernization of Doordarshan network by utilizing modern techniques and \nequipments is a continuous process. Schemes in this regard have been formulated \nand implemented from time to time. Modernization plan covers a broad spectrum, \nwhich inter-alia, includes digitalization , adoption of new technologies, upgradation \nand replacement of old ageing equipment etc.  \n*****�\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_111": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 111 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON NOVEMBER 23, 2016 \n \nWATER SCARCITY \n \nNo. 111 SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL; \n  SHRI PARVESH SAHIB SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of URBAN DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of proposals received by the Government from various \nStates for improving the water scarcity and laying of sewer pipelines; \n(b) the details of proposals cleared, funds sanctioned and works \nundertaken, project/State-wise; \n(c) whether the Government has conducted or proposed to conduct \nawareness programme to save and minimize usage of water and if so, \nthe details thereof; \n(d) whether the Government is facing hurdles in implementation of its \npolicies in various States including NCT of Delhi; and \n(e)  if so, the details thereof, State-wise and the steps taken to resolve \nsuch disputes/ hurdles? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER FOR URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n (SHRI M .VENKAIAH NAIDU) \n \n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n\n---2--- \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 111   FOR 23.11. 2016 REGARDING WATER SCARCITY. \n \n(a) & (b): The Govt. of India supports the efforts of State Governments \nfor Urban Development through various schemes and programmes.  The \nGovernment of India has received proposals under Atal Mission for \nRejuvenation and Urban Transformation (AMRUT), Urban Infrastructure \nDevelopment in Satellite Towns (UIDSST), 10% Lumpsum Scheme for \nNorth Eastern Region (NER) including Sikkim and one time special \nfinancial assistance for creation of essential urban infrastructure for the \nnew capital region of the State of Andhra Pradesh. These include \nproposals for improving the water supply and laying of sewer pipelines.  \n \n Under AMRUT, the Centre approves the State Annual Action Plans \n(SAAPs) submitted by the States. The State Govts concerned are \nempowered to prepare, appraise and approve the Detailed Projects \nReports (DPRs) for individual projects and thereafter implement the \nprojects. Details of proposals approved under Atal Mission for \nRejuvenation and Urban Transformation (AMRUT) under SAAP for 2015-\n16 and 2016-17 for water and sewerage projects  are at Annexure-I. The \ndetails of proposals approved and funds released under Urban \nInfrastructure Development in Satellite Towns (UIDSST) is at Annexure \n–II. The details of proposals approved and funds released under 10% \nLumpsum Scheme for North Eastern Region (NER) including Sikkim is at \nAnnexure-III. The details of proposals approved and funds released \nunder one time special financial assistance for creation of essential \nurban infrastructure for the new capital region of the State of Andhra \nPradesh is at Annexure-IV. \n \n(c) The AMRUT promotes recycle and reuse of treated waste water \nand also mandates the Urban Local Bodies to reduce the leakages and \nnon-revenue water. The activities related to such initiative are eligible \nto be taken up under the relevant component of project under AMRUT. \n \n\n(d) & (e): Government’s programmes for Urban Development including \nSmart Cities Mission, Swachh Bharat Mission, Atal Mission for \nRejuvenation and Urban Transformation are implemented in various \nStates and Union Territories including NCT of Delhi. Government is \nsupporting and monitoring the progress of implementation regularly to \nachieve the desired objectives under each Scheme/ Mission. NCT of \nDelhi is yet to submit the second annual action plan under AMRUT. All \nother States and Union Territories have submitted their second annual \naction plans which have been approved by the Ministry.  \n \n*****  \n \n  \n\n \n \n                                                                                                                                ANNEXURE-I \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a) and (b) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 111 for answer on 2\nNovember, 2016 \nFUND RELEASE UNDER ATAL MISSION FOR REJUVENATION AND URBAN TRANSFORMATION (AMRUT\n \nSl.N\no. \nState 2015-16 (Amount in Rs.Crore)  2016-17 (Amount in Rs.Crore) \nWater Supply Sewerage Water Supply Sewerage \nNo. of \nProjects \nProject \nCost \nNo. of \nProjects \nProject \nCost \nNo. of \nProjects \nProject \nCost \nNo. \nof \nPro\njec\nts \nProject C\no\n1 Andhra Pradesh 26 646.29  0 0.00 38 488.23  33 268.49 \n  A &N Islands 1 3.10  0 0.00 5 3.1  0 \n2 Arunachal \nPradesh \n1 5.00  3 23.00 1 8  1 \n3 Assam 3 151.48  3 31.95 1 213.2  0 \n4 Bihar 14 647.34    0.00 7 627.82  0 \n5 Chandigarh 2 13.66  1 1.00 1 17.55  0 \n6 Chhattisgarh 27 440.59  11 122.79 9 400.53  1 320.65 \n7 Dadra & Nagar \nHaveli \n1 3.33    0.00 1 3.51  0 \n8 Daman & Diu 0 0.00  1 4.44 0 0  1 5.75 \n9 Delhi 1 113.42  2 104.00 - - - - \n10 Goa 1 0.36  2 7.71 0 0  2 10.5 \n11 Gujarat 11 233.65  25 916.07 25 561  23 628.8 \n12 Haryana 15 227.07  7 200.00 16 166.59  16 219.67 \n13 Himachal \nPradesh \n7 41.65  6 37.28 9 25.27  6 20.31 \n14 Jammu & \nKashmir \n1 5.00  8 50.50 2 25.6  3 \n15 Jharkhand 5 190.00  6 116.36 5 236.5  5 130.8 \n16 Karnataka 45 551.54  61 612.65 22 726.44  20 727.24 \n17 Kerala 25 235.76  15 171.79 31 354.81  11 216.65 \n18 Lakshadweep 1 0.66  0 0.00 1 1.0208  0 \n19 Madhya \nPradesh \n27 582.33  23 925.65 28 678.76  25 1207.79 \n20 Maharashtra 21 1698.31  1 249.10 13 1466.39  11 963.52 \n21 Manipur 1 50.15  0   1 58.5  0 \n22 Meghalaya 0   2  22.22 0 0  1 \n23 Mizoram 4 7.30  2 7.30 3 15.5  1 3.67 \n24 Nagaland 1 4.00  1 2.50 1 4  0 \n25 Odisha 33 399.38  53 52.86 91 487  4 \n26 Puducherry 2 12.50  2 6.00 1 16.56  1 4\n27 Punjab 15 401.46  15 296.50 23 233.96  22 613.6 \n\n28 Rajasthan 10 344.00  6 555.00 8 252.05  10 786.25 \n29 Sikkim 0   0    1 2  0 \n30 Tamil Nadu 11 3147.04  1 33.71 7 1780.93  7 1974.42 \n31 Telangana 29 405.17  1 0.00 9 501.46  1 \n32 Tripura 3 33.20  1 2.50 3 43.1  1 \n33 Uttar Pradesh 60 1519.19  3