    "ls|LS16_S10_U_235": " \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*235 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2016 \n \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT IN TEXTILE SECTOR \n \n*235. SHRI MAHEISH GIRRI: \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंरी   \nbe pleased to state : \n \n(a)  whether her Ministry has initiated  skill development projects,                                    \nin textile sector, and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b)  the different sub -sectors of textile  industry such as apparel, handicrafts,  \nhandlooms, jute etc. included in the skill development projects; and \n \n(c)  the number of textile industry workers and labourers who have been  trained \nand the retention rate of the trained workers? \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र मंरी (                       ) \nMINISTER OF TEXTILES \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) to (c): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\n \n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( c) OF THE \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.* 235 TO BE ANSWERED ON \n01.12.2016. \n \n(a) & (b):   Yes, Madam. With a view to address the trained manpower needs of \nthe textile sector, the Government launched the Integrated Skill Development \nScheme (ISDS) in 2010 -11 as a pilot project and scaled up in 12 th Five Year Plan \nwith a target to train 15 lakh persons by 2016 -17. ISDS caters to the entire textile \nvalue chain and related segments of Handicrafts, Handlooms, Sericulture, Jute, \nTechnical Textiles etc. Government provides assistance to the extent of 75% of the \ntotal cost of the project within an overall ceiling of Rs. 10,000/ - per trainee. \nBesides ISDS, Development Commissioner (Handloom, Handicrafts), Central Silk \nBoard and such other organizations under the Ministry are also implementing \nskilling schemes under the respective Plan Schemes.   \n \n(c): As on 29.11.2016, a  total of about 8.61 lakh persons have been trained in  \nthe textile sector including traditional sectors under ISDS of which about 5.88 lakh \npersons have been provided placement. The average rate of placement of trainees \nunder ISDS in the industry is 69%. \n \n********** \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_236": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.236 \nANSWERED ON 17TH NOVEMBER, 2016 \n \nCONSTRUCTION OF ROADS FROM CENTRAL ROAD FUND \n \n236.  SHRI GANESH SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a)  whether the Central Road Fund  (CRF) is utilised for construction of roads,  if so, the \nnorms fixed for allotment of amount from the said fund; \n(b)  the State-wise details of the amount  accumulated and utilized under the CRF  during the \nlast three years; \n(c)  the names of the public representative from Madhya Pradesh on whose recommendations \namount has been sanctioned from the said fund during 2014 to 2016 for the construction of new \nHighways and the renovation of the existing highways in the State; and \n(d)  the names of the public  representative from Madhya Pradesh  whose recommendations \nunder the said scheme are pending with the Government from 2014 till date, and if so, the reasons \ntherefor and the time by which these recommendations will be accorded approval? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a) and (b)  The  funds  accrued under the Central Road Fund (CRF) during 2013-14, \n2014-15 and 2015 -16 were  Rs. 19,263 crore, Rs. 26,108 crore and Rs. 69,809 crore \n(provisional) respectively.  \n \n This Ministry is primarily responsible for development and maintenance of \nNational Highways (NHs).   \n \nThis Ministry allocates funds to the Stat es/Union Territories(UTs) for \ndevelopment of State Roads(Non -Rural Roads) under the CRF scheme.  The funds for \ndevelopment of State roads under CRF scheme are allocated to the States/UTs on the basis of \n30 percent weightage to fuel consumption and 70 percent weightage to the geographical area.  \nThe fund under CRF is non -lapsable.  Apart from this, the Ministry also allocates funds for \ndevelopment of State Roads of Economic Importance and Inter -State Connectivity (EI & \nISC) as per the CRF Act, 2000 amended by the Finance Act from time to time. The State/UT-\nwise details of the CRF accrual and releases and allocation and Release/Expenditure under EI \n& ISC schemes during the last three years is at Annexure-I & II respectively. \n \n(c) and (d) The identification, prioritisation and selection of projects for approval/sanction \nunder CRF and EI&ISC schemes are governed by the CRF( State Roads) Rules, 2014 \namended by the CRF( State Roads) Amendment Rules, 2016. Further, the limits of total \napproval of projects out of the proposal received from the  concerned State Governments in a \nparticular year is also governed by the said Rules.  \n \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) AND (b) OF LOK  SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 236 FOR ANSWER ON 17TH NOVEMBER, 2016 ASKED BY SHRI GANESH \nSINGH, MP REGARDING CONSTRUCTION OF ROADS FROM CENTRAL ROAD FUND \n \n(Rs. In crore) \nStatement showing State/UT-wise accrual and release during the last three years under CRF \nS. \nNo. \nName of the States/UTs 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \nAccrual Release* Accrual Release* Accrual Release \n1 Andhra Pradesh 197.24 197.24 225.15 #73.12 137.51 178.13 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 41.49 33.27 48.36 55.00 53.21 20.53 \n3 Assam 46.02 18.28 52.59 42.44 58.44 46.34 \n4 Bihar 64.38 45.47 73.73 83.36 81.70 80.88 \n5 Chhattisgarh 77.53 54.07 89.32 0.00 99.73 84.13 \n6 Goa 6.35 18.37 6.34 2.41 6.69 7.52 \n7 Gujarat 141.01 100.70 162.26 110.42 178.81 61.05 \n8 Haryana 66.42 66.42 73.73 73.73 80.25 79.45 \n9 Himachal Pradesh 32.19 24.80 37.26 0.00 41.00 95.14 \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 113.80 79.19 131.87 43.96 145.38 44.42 \n11 Jharkhand 51.46 46.14 59.46 17.74 66.29 9.10 \n12 Karnataka 138.06 138.06 160.14 170.51 179.98 164.46 \n13 Kerala 46.70 70.40 53.65 54.60 60.48 59.88 \n14 Madhya Pradesh 178.87 178.87 207.18 180.99 229.1