    "ls|LS16_S10_U_76": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.76 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 16.11.2016 \n \nUPGRADATION OF RAILWAY STATION \n \n76.  DR. SHASHI THAROOR: \n             \n Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether Kazhakootam  railway station (KZK) caters to the \ntechnopark, which employ s more than 40,000 Information \nTechnology (IT) professionals and the Vikram Sarabhi Space \nCenter; \n \n(b) if so, whether the Government would consider adopting measures \nto upgrade the station in collabo ration with the Ministry of \nCommunications and Information Technology with source funding \nfrom technopark companies; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(d) whether the Railways would consider upgrading KZK to 'Adarsh' \nstatus and ren ame the station to Kazhakootam - Technopark \nstation; and \n \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n(SHRI RAJEN GOHAIN) \n \n(a):  Yes, Madam.  Kazhakuttam is the nearest st ation to Technopark \nand Vikram Sarabhai Space Centre.   \n\n(b) & ( c): Upgradation of station is a continuous and on -going process \nand works in this regard are undertaken depending upon need, volume of \nthe passenger traffic and inter-se priority subject to availability of funds. \nHowever, no  such proposal has been received by Railways from the \nMinistry of Communication and Technology. \n(d) & (e): Kazhakuttam Railway station has been identified for \ndevelopment under Adarsh station Scheme and the related   works under \nthis scheme have been compl eted during 2015 -16.  As per the existing \nguidelines, Ministry of Home Affairs, Government of India, alone is the \ncompetent authority to approve change in the name of railway station on \nIndian Railways. It does so on the recommendation of the concerned \nState Government.   \n*** ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_77": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 77 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON NOVEMBER 16, 2016 \n \nSPORTS FACILITY IES IN SMART CITY MISSION \n \nNo. 77     SHRI RAVNEET SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of URBAN DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government propos es to provide ultra-modern \nsports facilities in selected smart cities; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Ludhiana Municipal Corporat ion has included \nthe construction of a similar sports facility under its smart-\ncity proposal; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the manner in which the \nremaining facilities can be included in smart cities? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN \nTHE MINITRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n(RAO INDERJIT SINGH) \n \n \n(a) & (b): No, Madam. The core infrastructure elements in a \nSmart City do not include modern sports facilities. However, \nif a particular smart city wants to develop modern sports \nfacilities, the Mission Guidelines do not preclude this. \n   \n(c):  No, Madam. Ludhiana Muni cipal Corporation has not \nproposed for construction of spo rts facility under its smart \ncity proposal. \n \n(d): Does not arise. \n \n………..  ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_78": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PLANNING \n \nLOK  SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 78  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 16.11.2016 \n \n \nSCHEME FOR THE DEVELOPMENT \n \n \n78. SHRI BHAIRON PRASAD MISHRA \n \nWill the MINISTER of PLANNING be pleased to state: \n \n \n(a) Whether Government propose to formulate any special schemes for the development of \ndistricts under the Bundelkhand backward area in the coming five year plan; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) if not, whether Government propose to consider any such scheme; and \n(d) if so, the details there of? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) MINISTRY OF PLANNING AND \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT AND  \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING & URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION \n \n(RAO INDERJIT SINGH) \n \n \n(a & b) No Madam, \n \n(c & d) Does not arise. \n \n \n----------  \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_79": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PLANNING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 79 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 16.11.2016 \n \nIMBALANCE IN THE PER CAPITA INCOME BETWEEN INTER-STATE STATES \n \n79. SHRI RAJU SHETTY:  \nWill the Minister of PLANNING be pleased to state: \n(a) whether there is imblance in the per capita income between inter-state and States;  \n(b) if so, the per-capita income of each state and the reasons therefor; and  \n(c) the steps taken by the Government to increase the per capita income of every State? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR MINISTRY OF PLANNING \nAND MINISTER OF STATE FOR MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT AND \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING & URBAN POVERTY ALLEVIATION \n  \n(RAO INDERJIT SINGH) \n \n \n(a) & (b): The per capita income level of States depend o n a number of factors which include \nresource endowments, historical background of development, availability of socio -economic and \nphysical infrastructure, demographic features and various other state specific factors. The \nState/UT-wise estimates of Per Capita Income (measured as Per Capita Net State Domestic Product \n(NSDP)) at current prices for the last three years are given at Annex.  \n(c): The Government has taken several measures to improve the growth performance of all the \nStates in a balanced manner. S ome of the policy initiatives in this direction include: transfer of \nresources from Centre to States favouring less developed States; incentives for setting up of \nindustries in the backward regions; etc. The enhanced share of States in Central taxes from 32 per \ncent to 42 per cent provides flexibility to the States for designing and financing schemes suited to \nthe local needs and to bridge the development gaps in the backward regions of the States through \nthe framework of co -operative and competitive federa lism. In addition, several Centrally \nSponsored Schemes and state -specific schemes are being implemented which are expected to \nimprove the per capita income of different States. \n \n*********** \n\nANNEX  \nAnnex as referred in reply to part (a) & (b) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 79 \nregarding Imbalance in the per capita Income between inter-state states for answer on \n16.11.2016 \nState/UT-wise Per Capita Income at current prices \nS. No. State\\UT 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \n1 Andhra Pradesh  84579 95689 107532 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 91990 101100 117178 \n3 Assam 49734 54618 NA \n4 Bihar 26948 31380 34168 \n5 Chhattisgarh 69863 78495 NA \n6 Goa 258856 274939 NA \n7 Gujarat 113139 124518 NA \n8 Haryana 136734 150260 NA \n9 Himachal Pradesh 114095 124500 NA \n10 Jammu & Kashmir 63202 65598 NA \n11 Jharkhand 50006 56737 62816 \n12 Karnataka 119160 132749 148485 \n13 Kerala 123387 139382 NA \n14 Madhya Pradesh 51639 56516 65388 \n15 Maharashtra 125146 134081 NA \n16 Manipur 47852 52436 NA \n17 Meghalaya 65118 67082 75232 \n18 Mizoram 77581 85659 NA \n19 Nagaland 71511 78526 NA \n20 Odisha 56941 63108 66890 \n21 Punjab 105143 114561 NA \n22 Rajasthan 69925 76881 NA \n23 Sikkim 194624 210394 227465 \n24 Tamil Nadu 116583 130197 143547 \n25 Telangana 114669 129182 143023 \n26 Tripura 61570 71666 NA \n27 Uttar Pradesh 40306 43861 48520 \n28 Uttarakhand 126957 134784 151219 \n29 West Bengal NA NA NA \n30 Andaman & Nicobar \nIslands \n109787 121954 NA \n31 Chandigarh 203377 225369 242386 \n32 Delhi 232269 252011 280142 \n33 Puducherry 148147 158830 172143 \nAll-India Per Capita NNI 79412 86879 93293 \nSource: Central Statistics Office \nNote: The estimates for the State West Bengal have not been finalized for new series with base \nyear 2011-12.   \n           NA: Not Available \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_8": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nDEPARTMENT OF ATOMIC ENERGY \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 8 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 16.11.2016 \n \nRADIATION PLANT \n \n \n*8.  SHRIMATI MAUSAM NOOR: \n \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes to introduce r adiation plants in selected locations \n in the country to check post-harvest losses and if  so, the details thereof; \n \n(b)  whether any requests/proposals have been invit ed by the Government in this regard  \n            and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether Russia has been selected in a JV to es tablish radiation plants in the country \n and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d) whether any incident of radioactive contaminati on has been reported in those \n countries that have availed this technology and if  so, the details thereof alongwith \n action taken by the Government to ensure fool proo f technology? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR, PERSONNEL, PUBLIC GRIEVA NCES AND PENSIONS \nAND PRIME MINISTER'S OFFICE (DR. JITENDRA SINGH): \n \n(a) & (b)  Yes, Sir.  As per Department of Atomic E nergy (DAE) vision for the next 15 years, \nBhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) is intending t o introduce radiation \nplants at several places in India in a phased manne r. These will efficiently reduce \nthe post-harvest losses of different types of rhizomes, spices and fruits. \n \n(c) The Department is not aware of any Joint Ventur e (JV) with Russia to be set up \nRadiation Plant. However, according to some print m edia sources, such JV with \nRussia has been planned by some private entrepreneu r.  However, setting up \nthese plants is subject to clearance of Atomic Ener gy Regulatory Board (AERB) \nand BARC.  \n Currently there are 15 food irradiation plants in India among this two have been \nset up by Government of India, one by Maharashtra G overnment and the rest 12 \nby private entrepreneurs. New Radiation Plants are going to be established by \nprivate entrepreneur with the help of Board of Radi ation and Isotope Technology \n(BRIT), AERB and BARC.  \n(d)   There is no report of radioactive contaminati on in food irradiation plants all over \nthe world.  For the plants in India, DAE ensures co mplete safety of the plants \nafter careful consideration of all safety aspects b y experts available in the \nDepartment and AERB.   \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_80": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 80 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 21.11.2016 \n \nKENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS AND NAVODAYA VIDYALAYAS \n \n†*80.  SHRI CHHEDI PASWAN:  \nSHRI J.C. DIVAKAR REDDY: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a)  the number of Kendriya Vidyal ayas (KVs) and Jawahar Navodaya \nVidyalayas (JNVs) functioning in the country, State/UT-wise; \n(b)  whether the Government has formul ated any criteria for opening of new \nKendriya Vidyalayas in the remote areas and if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  the details of proposals for open ing new KVs and JNVs  and strengthening \nthe existing areas particularly in the citi es and districts with a high density of \npopulation and if so, the details thereof during the last three years and the \ncurrent year and the reasons therefor; \n(d)  the time by which such schools are likely to be op ened, State/UT-wise \nalong with the amount of expenditure likely to be incurred thereon; and \n(e)  whether the Government intends to open separate KVs and JNVs for girls \nin the country and if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n (SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n(a) to (e) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n****** \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO  PARTS (a) TO (e) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 80 RAISED BY SHRI CHHEDI PASWAN & \nSHRI J.C. DIVAKAR REDDY , HON’BLE MEMBERS OF PARLIAMENT TO BE \nANSWERED ON 21st NOVEMBER, 2016 REGARDING KENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS \nAND NAVODAYA VIDYALAYAS. \n \n(a): The State/UT wise list of Kendriya Vidyalayas (KVs) and Jawahar Navodaya \nVidyalayas (JNVs) functioning in the country is annexed. \n(b) to (d): The KVs are opened primarily to  cater to the educational needs of the \nchildren of transferable Central Govern ment employees including Defence and \nPara–Military personnel by providing a common programme of education. Proposals \nfor opening of new KVs are considered onl y if sponsored by Ministries or \nDepartments of the Government of India / State Governments / Union Territories \nAdministrations and Organizati on of employees belonging to the eligible categories \nthereby committing resources for setting up a new KV as well as on the availability \nof necessary sanction of the Government.   \n The JNVs are established with the objec tive of providing good quality modern \neducation to the talented children, predomi nantly from the rural areas without regard \nto their family’s socio-economic c ondition. The Navodaya Vidyalaya Scheme \nprovides for opening of one Navodaya Vidyal aya in each district of the country. \nOpening of new JNVs is a continuous process which depends on the willingness of \nthe concerned State Government to make available requisite suitable land free of \ncost for construction of school buildi ngs and required temporary accommodation \n(free of rent) to start the Vidyalaya.  Actual sanction and  opening of new JNV \ndepends on the availability of funds and approval by Competent Authority.  \n(e): There is no such proposal under c onsideration at present. While, both KVs \nand JNVs are co-educational schooling system s, there is also a provision for \nreservation of at least 1/3\nrd seats for girl students in JNVs.  \n***** \n\nANNEXURE \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 80 RAISED BY SHRI CHHEDI PASWAN & SHRI J.C. DIVAKAR REDDY , \nHON’BLE MEMBERS OF PARLIAMENT TO BE ANSWERED ON 21 st NOVEMBER, 2016 \nREGARDING KENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS AND NAVODAYA VIDYALAYAS \n \nSTATE / UT-WISE LIST OF KENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS & JAWAHAR NAVODAYA \nVIDYALAYAS FUNCTIONING IN THE COUNTRY \n \nS. No.  Name of State/UT Number of KVs Number of JNVs \n1 A & N Islands 02 02 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 31 15 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 15 16 \n4 Assam 55 27 \n5 Bihar 47 39 \n6 Chandigarh 05 01 \n7 Chhattisgarh 28 17 \n8 Dadra & Nagar Haveli 01 01 \n9 Daman & Diu 01 02 \n10 Delhi 44 02 \n11 Goa 05 02 \n12 Gujarat 44 25 \n13 Haryana 29 20 \n14 Himachal Pradesh 25 12 \n15 Jammu & Kashmir 37 17 \n16 Jharkhand 32 24 \n17 Karnataka 45 28 \n18 Kerala 36 14 \n19 Lakshdweep 01 01 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 98 50 \n21 Maharashtra 57 33 \n22 Manipur 08 11 \n23 Meghalaya 07 08 \n24 Mizoram 04 08 \n25 Nagaland 05 11 \n26 Odisha 57 31 \n27 Puducherry 04 04 \n28 Punjab 49 21 \n29 Rajasthan 68 34 \n30 Sikkim 02 04 \n31 Tamil Nadu 42 00*  \n32 Telangana 32 09 \n33 Tripura 09 04 \n34 Uttar Pradesh 110 70 \n35 Uttarakhand 43 13 \n36 West Bengal 58 18 \nTOTAL 1136 594 \n \n\n* The State of Tamil Nadu has not yet accepted the JNV scheme. \n \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_81": "������������������� �\n������������������������ �\n�\n��������� �\n�������������������� ����� �\n�\n��� ��� ��������� ��� ���� �� �� ���������� ��������������� �� �� �����\n������ �\n�\n�������������������������������� �\n�\n����� ��� ������������������� �\n���������������� ������������������� �\n�\n������������������������������������������������������� �\n�\n���� �������� ���� ����������� ��� ����������� ���� �������� ��� ���������� ��� �\n���������������� �������� ���������� � ��������� ���������� ��� ����\n������������ ��������������������������������������� ����������������� �������\n��������������������� ����������������� �\n�\n���������������������������������� ��������� �\n�\n���� �������� ���� ������������ ����� ���� ���������� �������� ����������� ��� ��� �\n����������� ���� �� ������������ ����������� ���� ��������� �������� ���� ��� ����\n����������� �������� ��\n�\n����������������������������������� ����������������������������������������\n���� ������������������������������������������������� ��������������������������\n��� �\n�\n���������������������������������������������������������������� �\n�\n������ �\n�\n�������������������� ����������������������������� �\n������ ��������������������� ��\n�\n������������ ���������������������������������������������� �\n������ �\n�\n\n�\n�� ��\n�\n���������� ��������� ��� ��� ������ ��� ���� �� �� ������ ��������\n���������������������� ��� ������ �\n�\n���� �� ��� �� ������� ��������� ���������� ��� ���������������� ��� �����������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������� ��� ����� ����� ��� ���� ���� ����������� �������� ����� ����� ������\n������ ��� ������ ����� ��������� �������� ������ ��� ��������� ����� ����� �����\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������ �\n�\n������ ���������������������������������������������� ����������������������\n���� ������ ����������� ������ ��� ��������� ��������������� �������� ��� ����\n��������������������������������������������������������� ����������������\n���������������������������� �\n�\n����������� ��������� ��������������� ��� ��������� ������������� �� � ������\n��������������������������������������� �\n�\n���������",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_82": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS  \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 82 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 16.11.2016 \n \nFOOT OVER BRIDGE  \n \n†82. DR. BANSHILAL MAHATO:  \n  SHRI RAM TAHAL CHOUDHARY:  \n \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether there are n o Foot Over Bridges at the railway stations falling \nbetween Champa and Korba in Chhattisgarh and Namkum and Rai of \nRanchi district, Jharkhand; \n \n(b) if so, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) whether there is any proposal for construction of Foot Over Bridges at \nall the said stations; \n \n(d) if so, the time by which construction of the said Foot Over Bridges is \nlikely to commence; and \n \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS  \n \n (SHRI RAJEN GOHAIN ) \n \n(a) to (e): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n***** \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.82 BY DR. BANSHILAL MAHATO AND \nSHRI RAM TAHAL CHOUDHARY TO BE ANSWERED IN LOK SABHA \nON 16.11.2016 REGARDING FOOT OVER BRIDGE  \n(a) to (e):  Two Foot Over Bridges(FOBs) are available at Champa \nrailway station and one FOB is available at Korba railway station. \nThere are five railway stations viz. Balpur, Kothari Road, Madwarani, \nSaragbundia and Urga between Champa railway station and Korba \nrailway station, which are ‘E’ category stations and do not qualify for \nprovision of FOB as per norms of Minimum Essential Amenities. \nHowever, one FOB has been provided at Madwarani railway station. \nPresently, there is no proposal for provision of FOB at the o ther four \nstations. \n There is no station having name as Rai. However, there exists a \nstation having name as Ray in Ranchi District. There are sixteen \nrailway stations viz. Ranchi, Argora, Piska, Itky, Tangerbansali, \nNarkopi, Nagjua, Akashi, Irgaon, Loharda ga, Barkichampi, Barkakana, \nBhurkunda, Patratu, Tokisud and Hendigir between Namkom railway \nstation and Ray railway station.  FOBs are already existing at \nNamkom, Ranchi, Barkakana, Bhurkunda, Patratu and Ray railway \nstations. Lohardaga, being a single pla tform railway station does not \nrequire FOB. Rest of the stations are ‘E’ and ‘F’ category stations \nwhich do not qualify for provision of FOB as per norms of Minimum \nEssential Amenities. Presently, there is no proposal for provision of \nFOB at these stations . However, augmentation of amenities including \nFOBs at stations is a continuous process and is done as per traffic \nneeds, inter-se priority of works and availability of funds.  \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_83": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF FERTILIZERS \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 83*+ \nTO BE ANSWERED ON: 22.11.2016 \n \nFertilizer Plants in Foreign Countries \n \n+* 83   SHRI  JAI PRAKSAH NARAYAN YADAV: \n \n   Will the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether Indian entities are being encouraged to facilitate setting up of \nurea/fertilizer plants abroad and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether any agreement has been inked between Morocco and Krishak Bharati \nCooperative (KRIBHCO) for setting up of fertilizer plant and if so, the location \nidentified and the time by which production is likely to commence; \n(c) whether the Government has formulated any concrete action plan for reviving \nthe fertilizer plants lying closed in the country and if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) the present status thereof along with the closed fertilizer plants identified for \nrevival? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER FOR CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS AND PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS \n \n(SHRI ANANTH KUMAR) \n \n(a) to (d):   A statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n********* \n  \n\n-2- \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO +*83 TO BE ANSWERED ON 22.11.2016 REGARDING \nFERTILIZER PLANTS IN FOREIGN COUNTRIES \n------ \n \n(a): Yes Madam.  The Government has been encouraging Indian Companies to \nestablish Joint Ventures abroad in Countries which are rich in fertilizer resources for \nproduction facilities with buy back arrangements and to enter into long term agreement \nfor supply of fertilizers and fertilizer inputs to India. Further, the Department is also \nworking with the goal of having access to acquisition of the fertilizer raw materials \nabroad by Indian entities. The country is facing shortage  of natural gas, which is the \npreferred feed stock for production of nitrogenous fertilizers, a near total dependence on \nimports for Phosphatic fertilizer and its raw materials and full import dependence for \nPotash. \n \n(b):  Yes, Madam. KRIBHCO has signed a Term Sheet in mid of 2016 with a Moroccan \ncompany for setting up a NPK plant. The details are being worked out. The plant will be \nlocated in Nellore district of Andhra Pradesh. The production is expected by year 2019. \n  \n(c) & (d):  Yes, Madam. Government has approved to revive five closed fertilizer units of \nthe Fertilizer Corporation of India Ltd (FCIL)/Hindustan Fertilizer Corporation Ltd.(HFCL) \nby setting of new urea plants having capacity of producing 1.27 Million Metric Ton \n(MMT) Urea per annum at each of the five units namely Talcher, Ramagundam, \nGorakhpur and Sindri units of FCIL and Barauni unit of the HFCL. Pre- Project activities \nfor these units are currently going on. Unit wise Details and present Status of the revival \nplan are as under: \n \nTalcher Unit of FCIL: \n• Talcher Unit of FCIL is being revived on ‘Nomination basis’ by a Consortium of PSUs \nnamely RCF, GAIL, CIL and FCIL by setting up a coal based fertilizer plant of 1.27 \nMMTPA capacity.   \n• JV Company named “Talcher Fertilizers Limited (TFL)” has been formed. ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_84": " \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 84 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 16.11.2016 \n \nRECONSTITUTION OF ZONES AND DIVISIONS \n \n†84.  SHRI JUGAL KISHORE: \n                                                                 \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n  \n(a) whether the railways has reconstituted zones and Divisions in \nthe country or has taken any final decision on the recommendation \nmade by the committee set up for reconstitution; \n \n(b) if so, the detail s thereof and if not, the time by which a final  \ndecision is likely to be taken;   \n \n(c) whether the said committee has taken regional and \ngeographical components more into account than administrative as \nwell as operational components for ensuring universal \ndevelopment/extension of railways in all over the country; \n   \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and   \n  \n(e) if not, the steps taken/being taken by the railways for giving more \nimportance to regional/geographical components while taking decision \non the reconstitution?   \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n(SHRI RAJEN GOHAIN) \n \n(a) to (e) : A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n**** \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 84 BY SHRI JUGAL KISHORE TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 16.11.2016 REGARDING \nRECONSTITUTION OF ZONES AND DIVISIONS  \n \n(a) & (b):  Reorganisation of Indian Railways has been carried out from \ntime to time keeping in view the administrative and operational \nrequirements. A Committee was constituted in 2012 to examine requests \nfor creation of new zones and divisions . The Committee  in its report, \nconcluded that none of the requests for creation of new zones and \ndivisions is feasible.   \n \n(c) to (e): New Zones and Divisions on Indian Railways are set up keeping \nin view various factors like size, workload, accessibility, traffic pattern \nand other operational/ administrative requirements, consistent with the \nneeds of economy and efficiency. The Zones and Divisions on Indian \nRailways are neither based on the State/District boundaries nor are \ncreated on the basis of regional/geographical considerations. \nThe said Committee was constituted to analyse the large number of \nrequests, which had been received from various State Governments, \nUnion Ministers, Members of Parliament/MLAs, etc. for the creation of \nnew Zones and Divisions on Indian Railways, from various angles of \nrailway working like operational, financial, administrative and staff \nrelated issues etc. and examine the feasibility of creation of the proposed \nnew Zones/Divisions.  \nThe Committee had  submitted its report after examining the requests \nfrom these angles.  \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S10_U_85": "O.I.H.\n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, \nCOOPERATION\n \n& FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n85\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n22\nND\n \nNOVEMBER, 2016\n \n \n \nCOMPENSATION UNDER CROP INSURANCE SCHEME\n \n \n*85. SHRI KANTI LAL BHURIA: \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS \nWELFARE   \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \n½ãâ¨ããè\n  \n \nbe pleased to state\n:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe unit taken into consid\ne\nration for determining crop insurance and scale of \ncompensation provided under various crop insurance schemes; \n \n \n(b)\n \n \nthe extent to which the farmers are compensated by Insurance companies in the \nevent of damage or loss of crop due to natural calamities; \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether compensation under the various crop insurance schemes has been \nprovided to the farmers for damage of crops owing to hailstorm, excessive rainfall, storms, \netc. during the last year and the current year and if so, the details thereof, PSU and \nInsurance company\n-\nwise and State\n-\nwise; and\n \n \n(d)\n \n \nif not, the reasons therefor and the time\n-\nframe drawn for compensating farmers \nunder various crop insurance schemes?\n \nANSWER\n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n  \n½ãâ¨ããè\n \n \n \n \n  \n(SHRI RADHA MOHAN SINGH\n)\n \n \n \n(a) to (d\n):\n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (\nd\n) OF L\nOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n85\n \n \nDUE FOR REPLY ON\n \n22\nND\n \nNOVEMBER, 2016\n.\n \n \n(a):\n \nThe unit taken in\nto \nconsideration for determining Crop Insurance under Pradhan \nMantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY) is village/village panchayat or any other equivalent \nunit as notified by State Government for major crops. For other crops it may be a unit of \nsize of village/villa\nge panchayat or above.\n \n \nUnder Restructured Weather Based Crop \nInsurance Scheme (WBCIS) Reference Unit Area (RUA) is notified by the State \nGovernment based on available Automatic Weather Stations (AWSs)/Automatic Rain \ngauge Stations (ARSs) in the area.\n \nThe \nscale of compensation is based on the sum insured which under both the \nschemes is equal to Scale Of Finance per hectare as decided by the District Level \nTechnical Committee (DLTC) for relevant crop in that district.\n \n(b):\n \nIn the event of damage or loss of c\nrop due to natural calamities, \ncompensation/claim is determined based on the shortfall in actual yield as assessed \nthrough Crop Cutting Experiments (CCEs) vis\n-\na\n-\nvis threshold yield.  Threshold yield \nbeing average yield of past seven years (excluding a maxi\nmum two calamity year(s) as \nnotified by State Government/ Union Territory) multiplied by applicable indemnity level \nfor that crop.\n \nThe compensation/claim amount is arrived thereafter as per the formula below :\n \n(Actual Yield \n–\n \nThreshold\n \nYield\n) \n      \nX  Sum \nInsured\n \nThreshold Yield \n \nContd..2/\n-\n \n \n\n-\n2\n-\n \n(c) & (d)\n:  \n    \nAs per provisions of the schemes, the claims are being processed and \nsettled by the concerned insurance companies based on the final yield data/weather \ndata provided within the stipulated time by the State Government/designated agencies.   \nThe claims settl\ned by the insurance companies during Kharif 2015 and Rabi 2015\n-\n16  \nunder various crop insurance schemes are \nannexed\n.   \n \nFor the schemes implemented in 2015\n-\n16, State Government and Central \nGovernment were to provide equal subsidy towards premium and/or set\ntlement of \nclaims as per the relevant scheme. All proposals of premium subsidy and/or claims \nreceived from the implementing agency by the Central Government have been cleared.  \nFor current year Central Government has already initiated release of funds towa\nrds its \nshare of upfront advance premium subsidy.    Claims for the current year will however \nbe settled as per the time frame provided in the schemes, according to which cut\n-\noff \ndate for  receipt of yield data is within one month from final harvest.  Ther\neafter, the \nclaims are to be processed, approved and settled by the concerned insurance \ncompanies within three weeks from receipt of yield data.\n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE\n \n \nCLAIMS PAID UNDER NATIONAL AGRICULTURAL INSURANCE \nSCHEME DURING LAST YEAR\n \nRs. In lakh\n \n \n \nS.No.\n \nSTATE\n \n  \nKharif 2015\n \n \nRabi 2015\n-\n16\n \n1\n \nANDHRA PRADESH\n \n0\n \n0\n \n2\n \nASSAM\n \n0\n \n0\n \n3\n \nBIHAR\n \n0\n \n0\n \n4\n \nCHHATTISGARH\n \n67762\n \n0\n \n5\n \nGOA\n \n0\n \n0\n \n6\n \nGUJARAT\n \n179533\n \n18\n \n7\n \nHARYANA\n \n0\n \n0\n \n8\n \nHIMACHAL PRADESH\n \n162\n \n0\n \n9\n \nJHARKHAND\n \n0\n \n0\n \n10\n \nKARNATAKA\n \n69280\n \n0\n \n11\n \nKERALA\n \n0\n \n0\n \n12\n \nMADHYA PRADESH\n \n6\n \n0\n \n13\n \nMAHARASHTRA\n \n420505\n \n0\n \n14\n \nMANIPUR\n \n270\n \n0\n \n15\n \nMEGHALAYA\n \n0\n \n0\n \n16\n \nMIZORAM\n \n0\n \n0\n \n17\n \nODISHA\n \n0\n \n0\n \n18\n \nRAJASTHAN\n \n0\n \n0\n \n19\n \nSIKKIM\n \n0\n \n0\n \n20\n \nTAMILNADU\n \n784\n \n0\n \n21\n \nTELANGANA\n \n0\n \n0\n \n22\n \nTRIPURA\n \n0\n \n0\n \n23\n \nUTTAR PRADESH\n \n0\n \n0\n \n24\n \nUTTARAKHAND\n \n0\n \n0\n \n25\n \nWEST BENGAL\n \n0\n \n0\n \n26\n \nA & N \nISLANDS\n \n0\n \n0\n \n27\n \nPUDUCHERRY\n \n0\n \n0\n \n28\n \nJAMMU & KASHMIR\n \n0\n \n0\n \nGRAND TOTAL\n \n738302.82\n \n18.16\n \nNAIS \nwas implemented by Agriculture I\nnsurance Company of India \nLimited only\n.\n \n \nRs.466100 Lakh and Rs.177634 Lakhs have been released for \npayment to farmers  of Madhya \nPradesh  & Odisha for claims relating \nto  Kharif 2015 season\n.\n \n \n \n \n\n \nSCHEME\n \nMODIFIED AGRICULTURAL INSURANCE SCHEME (MNAIS)\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nCLAIMS PA